-## Subtopics
-
-### Sensitivity-Aware Vector Clustering
-Uses codebook indices for vectors. Choose when memory is the primary constraint and slight quantization error is acceptable. [Link](12167-sensitivity-aware-vector-clustering.md).
-
-### Compact 3D Gaussian Representation (C3DGS)
-Uses neural fields for color. Choose when view-dependent color is complex and can be approximated by a shared field. [Link](12927-compact-3d-gaussian-representation-c3dgs.md).
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Color Matrix Traversal:** Compressing the entire color matrix into ASCII strings is computationally expensive due to matrix traversal. Using `torch::where` and `torch::index_select` improves speed but may not be sufficient for full replacement of loops [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Line Breaks:** Using line break characters in the ASCII string adds overhead during writing and decoding; they should be avoided [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Value Range:** The integer component of the floating-point number is strictly limited to $[0, 94]$. Values outside this range require scaling via $d$ or are not supported by this specific encoding scheme [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Encoding/decoding is $O(1)$ per number, but matrix traversal for color compression is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of color entries. The source notes that even with optimized PyTorch functions, matrix traversal is a bottleneck [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Reduces the size of transmitted data. Integer compression reduces 4-8 bytes to 1 byte. Floating-point compression reduces 4-8 bytes to 2+ bytes (depending on $d$). Color matrix compression reduces 3-4 bytes per channel to 1 byte per value (due to 21 quantized levels) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Gains approximately 6% or less improvement in training speed [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, low overhead for individual numbers, effective for color matrices with limited dynamic range.
-*   **Limitations:** Not suitable for arbitrary large integers or floats without multi-character encoding (which increases complexity). Color matrix compression is slow due to traversal. Limited to specific value ranges [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When training speed is bottlenecked by data transmission and attributes fit within the specified ranges.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When high precision is required for all attributes, or when color values exceed $[0.0, 2.0]$.
+*   **Performance Gain:** Improves training speed by approximately 6% or less [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Data Size:** Significantly reduces the size of color matrix data by limiting precision to one decimal place (21 alternative numbers) and using ASCII compression [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Not suitable for compressing the entire color matrix efficiently due to traversal overhead. The floating-point encoding is restrictive regarding the magnitude of the integer component [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When training speed is bottlenecked by data transmission and the model allows for quantization of color values to 1 decimal place without perceptible quality loss [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in "I3DGS: Improve 3D Gaussian Splatting from Multiple Dimensions" by F. Author et al. (2024) [S1, unofficial]. It is part of a broader effort to optimize 3D Gaussian Splatting training by improving data transmission speed through compression. The authors found that while compression helps, the overhead of matrix traversal limits its effectiveness for color attributes.
+This method was proposed in the context of improving 3D Gaussian Splatting training speed from multiple dimensions, including compression [S1, unofficial]. It is part of the "I3DGS" research which explores various optimizations for the 3D Gaussian Splatting model [S1, unofficial].
 
